    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the Twelfth Dynasty ruled it from a new residence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itjtawy">Itjtawy</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisht">el-Lisht</a>.</li><li>Kings built a bureaucratic state, curbed provincial governors, reclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> and fortified the Nubian frontier at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna">Semna</a>.</li><li>Its literature and Middle Egyptian language became classics, studied by Egyptian scribes for some two thousand years.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhind-mathematical-papyrus">Rhind Mathematical Papyrus</a>, one of the main sources for Egyptian mathematics, was copied under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> from an original written in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

