    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii.jpg" alt="Mentuhotep II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Montuhotep_from_Deir_al-Bahri,_11th_Dynasty,_2065-2014_BCE;_Egyptian_Museum,_Cairo_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Mortel, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> grew out of the divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>, when a dynasty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herakleopolis-magna">Herakleopolis</a> claimed the throne in the north and a line of Theban rulers, the Eleventh Dynasty, built up power in the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a> (Nebhepetre), who came to the throne at Thebes around 2055 BCE, fought the Herakleopolitans and their allies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> and, probably within the first four decades of a reign of about fifty years, reunited the country; he marked the achievement by taking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> name Sematawy, 'he who unites the Two Lands'. His successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-iii">Mentuhotep III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-iv">Mentuhotep IV</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and sent expeditions to the quarries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hammamat">Wadi Hammamat</a> and, by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>. One expedition under Mentuhotep IV was led by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> named Amenemhat, who is generally identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-i">Amenemhat I</a>, founder of the Twelfth Dynasty around 1985 BCE, although how he came to power is unknown. Amenemhat I founded a new royal residence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itjtawy">Amenemhat-itj-tawy</a> ('Amenemhat seizes the Two Lands'), usually shortened to Itjtawy, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisht">el-Lisht</a> at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a>, moving the centre of government back towards the old Memphite heartland. The town itself has never been located, but the royal pyramids at Lisht mark its cemetery.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/byblos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byblos.jpg" alt="Byblos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byblos_Libanon_2003.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Orient at de.wikipedia, CC BY-SA 2.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The economy rested on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flood, on grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> farming and on the labour of a large peasantry, organised through royal domains, temples and private estates. The letters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heqanakht">Heqanakht</a>, a landowner of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a>, show a household managing fields, rents and rations of barley and emmer. The pyramid town at el-Lahun (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-lahun">Kahun</a>), built for the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-ii">Senusret II</a>, preserves a planned settlement of officials and workers, together with papyri on accounts, medicine, veterinary care and household lists. Kings of the late Twelfth Dynasty, above all Senusret II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>, developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> depression, controlling the waters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahr-yusuf">Bahr Yusuf</a> and bringing new land under cultivation around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shedet">Shedet</a>, cult centre of the crocodile god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobek">Sobek</a>. The state sent expeditions for stone, amethyst, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and turquoise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hammamat">Wadi Hammamat</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-el-hudi">Wadi el-Hudi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serabit-el-khadim">Serabit el-Khadim</a> in Sinai, and traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>; a caravan of Asiatic traders is painted in the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khnumhotep-ii">Khnumhotep II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beni-hasan">Beni Hasan</a>. In religion the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> at Abydos became a national focus, with many private stelae set up along its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/processional-way">processional way</a>, while the Coffin Texts extended to officials hopes of an afterlife once reserved for kings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/senusret-i.jpg" alt="Senusret I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_Senusret_I_Petrie_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W. M. Flinders Petrie (1853-1942), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> had no large standing army in the later sense: kings relied on royal retainers, levies raised through the provinces and Nubian auxiliaries, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjay">Medjay</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-desert">Eastern Desert</a>. Soldiers fought on foot with bows, axes, spears and hide shields; the horse and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> reached Egypt only after this period. Reunification was won by force, and a tomb near the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a> at Deir el-Bahri held the bodies of some sixty soldiers killed in battle. The main field of expansion was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> campaigned as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-cataracts">Second Cataract</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> led several expeditions south, fixing the frontier at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna">Semna</a> with boundary stelae that forbade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> to pass northwards except to trade, and clearing a canal through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-cataract">First Cataract</a> for his boats. A chain of mud-brick fortresses with bastions, ditches and loopholed walls, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buhen">Buhen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirgissa">Mirgissa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uronarti">Uronarti</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalfak">Shalfak</a>, Semna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumma">Kumma</a>, controlled the river and the desert routes and watched the growing kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> further south; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna-dispatches">Semna Dispatches</a>, copies of reports from these forts, record their patrols. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> the record is thinner, but the stele of the officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khusobek">Khusobek</a> mentions a campaign of Senusret III to a place called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Sekmem</a>, probably Shechem.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/instruction-of-amenemhat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/instruction-of-amenemhat.jpg" alt="Instruction of Amenemhat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/instruction-of-amenemhat">Instruction of Amenemhat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facs%C3%ADmilLasInstruccionesDelReyAmenemhatI_(46149914632)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rowanwindwhistler, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king stood at the head of a court and a bureaucracy that grew steadily in size and specialisation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> oversaw justice and administration, the treasurer controlled stores and expeditions, and scribes kept detailed records of land, labour and rations, as the papyri from el-Lahun and the Reisner papyri show. The Twelfth Dynasty is thought to have secured the succession through co-regencies, the reigning king associating his heir on the throne, although how formal this practice was is still debated. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> powerful provincial governors, the nomarchs, ruled their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nomes</a> with near-royal display, as the rock-cut tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beni-hasan">Beni Hasan</a> and the scene of a colossal statue being hauled in the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djehutyhotep">Djehutyhotep</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bersha">Deir el-Bersha</a> show. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> such great provincial tombs disappear, which is usually explained as a curbing of the nomarchs in favour of central offices, with the country run through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> for the north, the south and the 'head of the south'. Royal ideology was spread through literature: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/instruction-of-amenemhat">Instruction of Amenemhat</a>, in which the dead king warns his son after an attack on his life, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loyalist-instruction">Loyalist Instruction</a> taught service to the crown. Royal statues also changed, showing the heavy-lidded, careworn faces of Senusret III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/white-chapel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/white-chapel.jpg" alt="White Chapel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/white-chapel">White Chapel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Chapel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Markh at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> looked back on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> as a classical age. Its language, Middle Egyptian, remained the standard for formal inscriptions and religious texts down to Roman times, and its literature was copied in scribal training for centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tale-of-sinuhe">Tale of Sinuhe</a>, the story of a courtier who flees to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-i">Amenemhat I</a> and returns home in old age, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tale-of-the-eloquent-peasant">Tale of the Eloquent Peasant</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tale-of-the-shipwrecked-sailor">Tale of the Shipwrecked Sailor</a> and the Dialogue of a Man and his Ba are among the earliest masterpieces of narrative and reflective writing. Mathematical texts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-mathematical-papyrus">Moscow Mathematical Papyrus</a> and the original of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhind-mathematical-papyrus">Rhind Mathematical Papyrus</a>, which was copied under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> from a text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>'s reign, preserve Egyptian arithmetic and geometry. Jewellery from the burials of royal women at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a> and el-Lahun, such as the pectorals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sithathoriunet">Sithathoriunet</a>, ranks among the finest Egyptian metalwork. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> still stands at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heliopolis-ancient-egypt">Heliopolis</a>, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-chapel">White Chapel</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> was rebuilt in modern times from blocks reused in a later pylon. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> was worshipped as a god in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, and Greek writers knew Amenemhat III's mortuary temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawara">Hawara</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv.jpg" alt="Amenemhat IV" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv">Amenemhat IV</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amenemhat_IV_upclose.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Juan R. Lazaro, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Twelfth Dynasty ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv">Amenemhat IV</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobekneferu">Sobekneferu</a>, the first woman known for certain to have taken the full titles of a king, around 1773 BCE. The Thirteenth Dynasty that followed kept the residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itjtawy">Itjtawy</a> and much of the administration, and under kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neferhotep-i">Neferhotep I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobekhotep-iv">Sobekhotep IV</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> still built, quarried and received the homage of the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>. Yet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin-king-list">Turin King List</a> records several dozen kings in about a century and a half, many with reigns of a few years or less, which suggests that real power lay increasingly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> and court families. Meanwhile people of Levantine origin had settled in the eastern Delta around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> (Tell el-Dab'a), and a separate line, the Fourteenth Dynasty of Egypt, ruled there in the eighteenth century BCE, from a date estimated between about 1805 and 1725 BCE. In the far south the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-cataracts">Second Cataract</a> forts slipped out of Egyptian control, and several later came under the Nubian kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a>. Around 1650 BCE the rulers known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>, the Fifteenth Dynasty of Egypt, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memphis-egypt">Memphis</a> and the north, and the residence at Itjtawy was abandoned. Egypt was divided among the Hyksos, a Theban line in the south and probably a short-lived Abydos Dynasty, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a>, until Theban kings reunited the country and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a> and created the classical forms of Egyptian language, literature and kingship, while its bureaucracy, its reclamation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> and its fortified frontier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> set models that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> consciously followed.</em></p>

